WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:06.100
61,000 cars cross this bridge every day.

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:08.400
Nobody sees what holds it up.

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:10.800
It's down here.

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:15.800
To check it, a diver goes down into waters so murky they often work by touch.

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:19.600
Massachusetts has 778 bridges that need this.

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:22.600
MassDOT has 7 divers.

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:27.800
And scour is worst in a flood, exactly when nobody can go in.

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:32.100
Federal rules keep the diver, sonar can only supplement.

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:39.700
So we asked, what if a robot went first and divers only went where the map says?

00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:43.800
In one day, from scratch, we built it in NVIDIA Isaac Sim.

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:51.000
A digital twin of this crossing, flown by ArduPilot, the same flight code that runs on real hardware.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:52.400
Above the water, a drone.

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:58.000
Its lidar fires 128 laser beams and turns the bridge into millions of 3D points.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:03.000
A gimbal camera and a thermal camera simulated here hunt for cracks and damp stone, floodlights

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:08.200
for the underside, RTK GPS for position.

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:12.400
Below, a BlueROV2. There is no GPS underwater.

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:14.000
So it navigates by sound.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:18.400
A Doppler velocity log pings the riverbed and tracks its own speed, 40 centimeters of

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:20.600
drift after 60 meters.

00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:23.700
A multibeam sonar sees where the camera sees only silt.

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:27.200
Eight thrusters hold it in the current.

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:30.000
The mission: the drone takes the wall.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.600
Under the deck, 12 satellites become seven.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:37.600
At the waterline it spots damage and hands the spot to the robot below.

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:40.200
The ROV dives straight to the mark.

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:44.500
Its sonar finds what nobody could see, a missing granite block, a scour pit, timber

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:47.300
piles exposed.

00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:58.400
Four flagged, four inspected, one model above the water and below.

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:02.600
Then the brain, built with Kiro: it finds the defects, cites every one in the report

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:05.000
and waits for an engineer to sign off.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:07.100
Every finding lands in one dashboard.

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:13.400
The 3D map, the camera footage, the GPS track and a report that Kiro writes itself.

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:18.800
Robot first is 20 to 30 percent cheaper per bridge by our estimate on a $16,000 kit.

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:22.500
By 2028, every state must recode its scour data.

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:26.100
And the pilot money already exists.

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:27.600
Today it's a simulation.

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:32.000
Next, a 10-bridge pilot checked against divers.

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:34.300
Robot first, divers only where the map says.
